## Current state vs. ticket (2026-05-11 refresh)

What 0116 actually chose: `ParseDiagnostics` is a separate struct returned
alongside `Erg`. The `Erg` struct in `src/go/erg.go:19-33` is already free
of diagnostic-trace fields — only schema/body/log content remain. The
elimination target for 0117 is therefore the **`ParseDiagnostics` struct
itself** (defined `erg.go:35-46`), not fields on `Erg`.

Other intermediate PRs (since the ticket was written) that affect the plan:
- PR #126 split `parseErg` into a thin reader wrapper plus
  `parseErgBytes(data []byte, path string)` (the real walker,
  `erg.go:202-331`). All line-by-line work happens in `parseErgBytes`.
  `parseErg` is now a 5-line wrapper (`erg.go:185-191`).
- PR #128 unexported `magicLine`, `isoDateRE`, `filenameRE`, `logLineRE`,
  `validTagValues` into `ergspecv1.go`; added `hasHeaderKey` helper used
  by `isClosedHeaderLine`. Affects nothing for this ticket but the helper
  is the right point to capture line numbers when emitting "Closed in log"
  errors mid-walk.
- PR #124 added line-number-aware error messaging hooks (review at execute
  time — the line-tracking infrastructure may already exist).

Tally of touched call sites (current main):
- `parseErg(` direct callers (non-test): none outside erg.go. Production
  callers use `loadErgs` (4 sites: archive.go:63, check.go:132,
  close.go:125, ready.go:201) or `parseErgBytes` (1 site: close.go:83).
- `validateErg(` callers: only `validateAll` (validate.go:237) and
  validate_test.go (6 direct calls — these are the ones to rewrite).
- `loadErgs(` callers: 4 production + 15 in tests; signature change here
  ripples wider than `parseErg`.
- Tests reading `diag.HasLogSep`/`HasBodySep`/`ClosedInBody` directly:
  6 sites in erg_test.go (lines 59, 62, 95, 98, 134, 188, 200) and 2 in
  validate_test.go (lines 457, 460). These tests assert on the
  diagnostic-trace field directly today; under 0117 they assert on the
  emitted error string instead.

## Context
After ticket 0116, the parser emits a `ParseDiagnostics` struct
(`Unknown`, `RepeatedSingletons`, `ClosedEmpty`, `ClosedInLog`,
`ClosedInBody`, `HasLogSep`, `HasBodySep`) that exists only so the
separate validator can re-derive what the parser already saw. This is
two passes over the same information.

A validating parser emits parse errors directly as it walks the file.
`ParseDiagnostics` collapses to `[]string`. `parseErgBytes` becomes the
single source of truth for both shape and error reporting.

## Proposed signature change
The work lives in `parseErgBytes`; `parseErg` inherits the new shape:

```go
// Before (current main, post-#126)
func parseErg(path string) (Erg, ParseDiagnostics)
func parseErgBytes(data []byte, path string) (Erg, ParseDiagnostics)
func validateErg(t *Erg, diag ParseDiagnostics, allIDs map[string]bool) []string

// After
func parseErg(path string) (Erg, []string)
func parseErgBytes(data []byte, path string) (Erg, []string)
// Corpus-level checks stay separate — they need the full corpus.
func validateCorpus(tickets []Erg, parseErrs [][]string) []string
```

`loadErgs` signature changes in lockstep: `([]Erg, [][]string)` instead
of `([]Erg, []ParseDiagnostics)`. The four production callers
(archive/check/close/ready) currently ignore the diagnostics slice with
`_`, so the rename is a one-token edit per call site.

**Rule split** (using validator's current numbering in helpValidate):
- Per-file rules into parser: 1, 2, 3, 3a, 4 (Closed empty/misplaced),
  5, 6, 7 (filename), 8 (single-file Blocked-by parse), 9, 11
  (separators), 12 (date format).
- Corpus rules stay in `validateCorpus`: 7-dup (duplicate IDs across
  corpus), 10 (local Blocked-by ID resolution), 13 (dependency cycles).

`[]string` return matches `validateAll`'s current return type. A
`ParseError` struct (file/line/rule fields) is YAGNI — no consumer
reads errors programmatically today.

## Error message format (split as follow-up — see Out of scope)
Upgrading from `"filename: msg"` to `"filename:LINE: msg"` is orthogonal
to the merge and can land before, after, or as part of this ticket.
Recommendation: do the merge first against the existing format, then
file a follow-up that walks one rule at a time adding line numbers.
Rationale: the merge already touches every parser/validator test fixture;
piling on the format change doubles the diff and entangles two
independent rollbacks.

If kept in scope: end-of-file errors (e.g. "missing `--- log ---`
separator") omit the line number; convention is `"filename: msg"` when
no specific line applies, `"filename:LINE: msg"` otherwise. Tests
mostly use `strings.Contains` for error assertions, so impact is
narrower than feared but still volume-y.

## Actions (incremental migration)

**Step 1 (redesigned).** The naive "flip the second return type, return
nil" cannot compile: `validateErg` and ~25 destructure sites consume
`ParseDiagnostics`. Three viable transitional shapes; pick one and
commit:

  - **(a) Parallel function.** Introduce
    `parseErgFull(data, path) (Erg, []string)` alongside the existing
    `parseErgBytes` returning `ParseDiagnostics`. Migrate one trace
    field at a time from the old function to the new. Delete the old
    function once all fields have moved. *Largest LOC churn, simplest
    per-commit diff.*
  - **(b) Triple return transitional.** `parseErgBytes` returns
    `(Erg, ParseDiagnostics, []string)`. Each migration commit moves
    one error emission from the validator into the parser, populating
    `[]string` and zeroing the matching `ParseDiagnostics` field.
    Final commit drops the middle return. *Compiles cleanly at every
    step but every call site is touched twice.*
  - **(c) Embed []string in ParseDiagnostics.** Add `Errors []string`
    to `ParseDiagnostics`. Migrate field-by-field as before, but the
    parser appends to `diag.Errors` and the validator stops checking
    the trace flag. Final commit renames `ParseDiagnostics` to a
    type alias or drops the struct in favour of plain `[]string`.
    *Smallest per-commit diff; ugliest intermediate type.*

Recommendation: **(c)**. The intermediate ugliness is short-lived (one
PR) and every commit touches the minimum number of files. Explicitly
record the choice in the first commit message.

**Steps 2-9 (unchanged in intent, renumbered where needed):**

2. Migrate `HasMagic` rule — parser emits "missing magic first line"
   directly into the errors slice; validator stops checking `t.HasMagic`.
   (Note: `HasMagic` is a field on `Erg`, not on `ParseDiagnostics`. It
   stays on `Erg` if any caller still needs it post-merge; otherwise
   delete it too in a follow-up.)
3. Migrate `ClosedInLog` / `ClosedInBody` — parser emits at the
   `isClosedHeaderLine` site.
4. Migrate `Unknown` / `RepeatedSingletons` — parser emits at the
   header-classification branch.
5. Migrate `ClosedEmpty` — parser emits at the `Closed:` branch.
6. Migrate `HasLogSep` / `HasBodySep` — parser emits at end of walk
   (these rules only know the answer after the full pass).
7. Delete `validateErg` after all per-file rules have migrated; delete
   `ParseDiagnostics` struct.
8. Rename `validateAll` to `validateCorpus`; update `cmdValidate`
   (validate.go:333) and `cmdCheck` (check.go:138) call sites. Also
   update `loadErgs` signature: `([]Erg, [][]string)`. Production
   callers in archive.go/close.go/ready.go that currently discard the
   second return with `_` keep the underscore — no other changes.
9. Update `validate_test.go` (6 direct `validateErg` calls,
   2 `diag.*` assertions) and `erg_test.go` (6 `diag.*` assertions).
   Test rewrite is mechanical: `errs := parseErg(path); contains(errs,
   "expected message")` replaces `diag, _ := parseErg(...); if
   !diag.HasFoo { ... }`.

## Leniency / recovery
The parser is **lenient**: it never bails on a single error. It walks
the whole file, accumulates every error it can find, returns the
best-effort `Erg` alongside the `[]string`. Same policy as today's
parser; restate explicitly so the implementer doesn't "fix" it.

- Malformed header line → emit, skip line, continue.
- Unknown header → emit migration hint, continue.
- Missing magic → emit, attempt header parse anyway.
- Missing separator → emit at end, body content stays wherever the
  parser was.
- Empty required header → emit, continue.

The validator's exit code is non-zero if any error fired; strictness
lives at the CLI level, not the parser level.

## Migration test contract
`make test` must stay green at every commit in the incremental
migration (step 1 transitional shape → field-by-field). Each
per-commit diff keeps tests passing; the final commit deletes
`validateErg` and `ParseDiagnostics`. No "green only at the end"
merges.

End-of-walk emissions: rule 4 (Closed in log/body) and rule 11
(separator missing) only know the answer at end of file. They emit
before return, not mid-walk. Flag for the implementer.

`validate_test.go` callers of `validateErg` directly (6 sites at
v_t.go:219, 355, 388, 442, 483, 506): rewrite to call `parseErg` and
assert on its `[]string`. Mechanical but volume-y.

## Riskiest step + mitigation
**Step 6** (HasLogSep / HasBodySep migration) is the highest-risk
single commit. Reasons:
  1. End-of-walk emission requires reordering the parser's return
     statement and any early-return paths (currently the only early
     return is on `os.ReadFile` error, but step 1 may have added more).
  2. The "separator inside body is body text" rule (rule 12 relaxation
     from 0116) lives in this code path. A naive migration risks
     re-introducing the old over-strict behaviour.
  3. Tests at erg_test.go:59,62,95,98,134 and validate_test.go:457,460
     directly read `diag.HasLogSep`/`HasBodySep` — they must be
     rewritten to assert on the absence of the missing-separator error
     string, not the presence of a positive boolean. The semantic
     inversion is a footgun.

Mitigation: handle step 6 as its own PR with a pre-commit run of the
full test suite plus a corpus-level smoke (`erg check tickets/` and
`erg check tickets/closed/`) before merging. Keep the migrated boolean
as a local variable in `parseErgBytes` until the very last line of the
function so the rule-12 logic remains lexically adjacent to the
emission.

## Open question
Eliminating ~7 trace fields requires coupling error policy into the
parser. The "duplication" between parse-and-validate may actually be
cheaper than the merge. Reconsider during execution if the per-commit
diffs feel forced. **Refresh note:** the call-site tally above
(production callers all use `_` for diagnostics) suggests the merge
costs even less than the original estimate — fewer than a dozen lines
of non-test production code change.

## Out of scope
- Type tightening (`BlockedBys []Ref`, `Created time.Time`). Filed
  separately so this ticket stays focused on the pass merge.
- Changes to validator rules themselves; only the architecture moves.
- **Error message format upgrade** (`filename:LINE: msg`). Split as
  follow-up ticket — orthogonal to the merge, doubles test churn if
  bundled in. The merge lands against today's `"filename: msg"`
  format; the format upgrade lands on top.

## Dependencies
- Hard dep: ticket 0116 (schema-literal struct). Closed
  2026-05-11; `ParseDiagnostics` exists, `Erg` is clean of trace
  fields. Dependency satisfied.

## Exit criteria
- `ParseDiagnostics` struct deleted from `erg.go`. (`Erg` was already
  clean of trace fields post-0116; this ticket finishes the cleanup
  on the diagnostics side.)
- `parseErg` and `parseErgBytes` return `(Erg, []string)`.
- `loadErgs` returns `([]Erg, [][]string)`.
- Per-file rules emit errors at parse time, not in a separate pass.
- Corpus-level rules (duplicate IDs, ref resolution, cycles) live in
  a clearly-named `validateCorpus`.
- `make test` passes; error messages keep today's `"filename: msg"`
  format (format upgrade is a separate ticket).
- No direct reads of `diag.*` fields remain in tests; assertions are
  on error strings.
